# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets redirect rules.
#
# Production replacement for `redirects()` in next.config.mjs, which a static export
# cannot run. Copied into `out/` by the build; evaluated by the asset router before any
# asset lookup, so these cost no Worker invocation.
#
# 308, not 301: 301 lets an agent downgrade a POST to GET on replay. Nothing POSTs to
# these paths today, but 308 is the permanent redirect that preserves method, and it is
# what `permanent: true` emitted before the migration — keeping the status identical
# means no already-indexed URL changes its meaning.
#
# Canonicalises the mockup's /plans routes onto /esim/:slug (blueprint §28.3). Mirrored
# in next.config.mjs for `next dev`; change both together.

# MUST STAY FIRST. `_redirects` is evaluated before asset matching and first match wins,
# so the `/destinations/:slug` rule below was swallowing Next's own route-payload files —
# `/destinations/__next._tree.txt` and six siblings are real files in `out/destinations/`,
# but `:slug` matched them and 308'd each to `/esim/__next._tree.txt`, which does not
# exist. Every one 404'd.
#
# Measured cost of that, mobile / 4x CPU / Slow 4G, clicking "Destinations" from the home
# page: 1273 ms, versus 95 ms for a country page whose prefetch was intact — 13x. The
# payloads are what the App Router prefetches, so losing them turns an instant client-side
# transition into a fetch-on-demand stall. No country slug can begin with `__next.`, so
# this rule cannot shadow a real destination.
/destinations/__next.*  /destinations/__next.:splat  200

/plans                  /destinations   308
/plans/:slug            /esim/:slug     308
/destinations/:slug     /esim/:slug     308
